      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/group**",
          "```\n├── study1/\n│   ├── analysis.R  💻 code    ex1\n│   └── data.csv    📊 data    ex1\n├── study2/\n│   └── data.csv    📊 data    ex2\n├── LICENSE         ❓ unknown\n└── README.md       📄 readme\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## data.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:5, y = c(2.2, 1.3, 4.1, 3.8, 6.5)), row.names = c(NA, \n5L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## data.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"id\", \"id\", \"y\", \"y\"), Representation = c(\"text\", \n\"text\", \"numeric\", \"numeric\"), Level = c(\"nominal\", \"nominal\", \n\"ratio\", \"ratio\"), Concept = c(\"id\", \"id\", \"\", \"\"), Role = c(\"identifier\", \n\"identifier\", \"measure\", \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"\", \"\", \"\", \"\"), \n    Rows = c(5L, 5L, 5L, 5L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0, 0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(5L, \n    5L, 5L, 5L), Mean = c(NA, NA, 3.38, 3.58), SD = c(NA, NA, \n    1.768, 1.997), Min = c(NA, NA, 1.2, 1.3), Max = c(NA, NA, \n    5.5, 6.5)), row.names = c(\"id...1\", \"id...2\", \"y...3\", \"y...4\"\n), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 4 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 1 data file could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
      },
